" (https://d2tic4wvo1iusb.cloudfront.net/documents/projects/EEF_Project_Report_MathematicsMasteryPrimary.pdf?v=1630926115)\nciteturn19view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 8 months ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 34; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://d2tic4wvo1iusb.cloudfront.net/documents/projects/EEF_Project_Report_MathematicsMasteryPrimary.pdf?v=1630926115\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1089\nL0@P0: Education\nL1@P0: Endowment\nL2@P0: Foundation\nL3@P0: Mathematics Mastery\nL4@P0: Primary Evaluation Report\nL5@P0: February 2015\nL6@P0: Independent evaluators:\nL7@P0: Anna Vignoles\nL8@P0: John Jerrim\nL9@P0-1: Richard Cowan Mathematics Mastery: Primary\nL10@P1: Education Endowment Foundation 1\nL11@P1: The Education Endowment Foundation (EEF)\nL12@P1: The Education Endowment Foundation (EEF) is an independent grant-making charity dedicated to\nL13@P1: breaking the link between family income and educational achievement, ensuring that children from all\nL14@P1: backgrounds can fulfil their potential and make the most of their talents.\nL15@P1: The EEF aims to raise the attainment of children facing disadvantage by:\nL16@P1:  Identifying promising educational innovations that address the needs of disadvantaged\nL17@P1: children in primary and secondary schools in England;\nL18@P1:  Evaluating these innovations to extend and secure the evidence on what works and can be\nL19@P1: made to work at scale;\nL20@P1:  Encouraging schools, government, charities, and others to apply evidence and adopt\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://ffteducationdatalab.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/Mathematics-Mastery-KS2.pdf)\nciteturn19view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 12; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://ffteducationdatalab.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/Mathematics-Mastery-KS2.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 303\nL0@P0: Mathematics Mastery evaluation, Key Stage 2, 2018-19\nL1@P0: Report from FFT Education Datalab\nL2@P0: to Mathematics Mastery\nL3@P0: Natasha Plaister\nL4@P0: Dave Thomson\nL5@P0-1: June 20211 Executive summary\nL6@P1: 1.1 Methodology\nL7@P1:  This report evaluates the effect of signing up to the Mathematics Mastery primary\nL8@P1: programme, as measured by scaled score in Key Stage 2 mathematics tests.\nL9@P1:  We use an intention-to-treat analysis: we evaluate the effect on all schools that\nL10@P1: signed up to participate, regardless of the extent to which they implemented the\nL11@P1: programme in practice.\nL12@P1:  We look at the effect on this outcome in the 2017/18 and 2018/19 academic years.\nL13@P1:  Our analysis used data from the National Pupil Database (NPD) to compare the\nL14@P1: performance of pupils in schools that took part in the project to the performance of\nL15@P1: pupils in a matched group of comparison schools.\nL16@P1:  Multilevel regression models were fitted to the data, with an indicator to flag\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nArk Mathematics Mastery - first trial | EEF (https://educationendowmentfoundation.org.uk/projects-and-evaluation/projects/mathematics-mastery-3)\nciteturn19view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn17view1\",\"pattern\":\"months\"}); Total lines: 132\nL1: # Ark Mathematics Mastery – first trial\nL2: \nL3: The Ark Mathematics Mastery programme is a whole-school approach aiming to deepen pupils’ conceptual understanding of key mathematical concepts.\nL4: \nL5: ARK\nL6: \nL7: Implementation cost The cost estimates are based on the average cost of delivering the intervention.\nL8: \nL9: Evidence strength This rating provides an overall estimate of the robustness of the evidence, to help support professional decision-making in schools.\nL10: Impact (months)The impact measure shows the number of additional months of progress made, on average, by children and young people who received the intervention, compared to similar children and young people who did not.\nL11: \nL12: +1\nL13: \nL14: months\nL15: \nL16: Project info\nL17: ### Independent Evaluator\nL18: \nL19: 【1†Image: UCL Institute of Education logo†d2tic4wvo1iusb.cloudfront.net】\nL20: \nL21: UCL Institute of Education\nL22: \nL23: The Ark Mathematics Mastery programme is a whole-school approach aiming to deepen pupils’ conceptual understanding of key mathematical concepts.\nL24: \nL25: Schools: 127 Grant: £774,000\nL26: \nL27: Key Stage: 3 Duration: 2 year(s) 2 month(s) Type of Trial: Effectiveness level evidence\nL28: \nL29: Completed December 2014\nL30: Mathematics Mastery by Ark Curriculum Plus is a whole-school approach to teaching mathematics. It aims to raise attainment for all pupils and close the attainment gap between pupils from low-income families and their peers. The programme aims to deepen pupils’ conceptual understanding of key mathematical concepts.\nL31: Compared to traditional curricula, fewer topics are covered in more depth, and greater emphasis is placed on problem solving and on encouraging mathematical thinking. To help schools and teachers make this shift there is training and in-school support, an online toolkit for teachers, and collaboration amongst teachers delivering the approach.\nL38: Our trial of the Ark Mathematics Mastery Primary School programme involved 5,108 pupils in 90 schools. The independent evaluation found that pupils in schools adopting Ark Mathematics Mastery made, on average, two months’ progress compared to other pupils in comparison schools. These results have a moderate-to-high security rating: 3 out of 5 on the EEF padlock scale.\nL40: Our trial of the Ark Mathematics Mastery Secondary School programme involved 7,712 pupils in 40 schools. The independent evaluation found that pupils in schools adopting Ark Mathematics Mastery made, on average, one months’ progress compared to other pupils in comparison schools. These results have a high security rating: 4 out of 5 on the EEF padlock scale. A similar average impact was found for pupils eligible for free school meals.\nL48:   2. There were 90 schools involved in the primary school trial, mainly in London and the South East.\nL49:   3. There were 50 schools involved in the secondary school trial, the majority of which were in London and the South East with a significant minority in the Midlands.\nL50:   4. Participating schools could not be already receiving the Ark Mathematics Mastery programme.\nL51:   5. Headteachers, maths coordinators and class teachers received one or two days of launch training and two in-school development visits.\nL52:   6. Teachers attended three cluster workshops with teachers from other schools that are implementing the same curriculum.\nL53:   7. Teachers had access to an online toolkit which includes mastery-aligned ​‘lesson designs’, continuous professional development resources, and assessments.\nL54:   8. The programme is available nationwide through Ark Curriculum Plus.\nL55: Outcome/​Group\nL56: \nL57: Impact The size of the difference between pupils in this trial and other pupils\nL58: \nL59: Security How confident are we in this result?\nL60: \nL61: Overall impact\nL62: \nL63: +1\nL64: \nL65: Months' progress\nL66: \nL67: Primary maths (Year 1)\nL68: \nL69: +2\nL70: \nL71: Months' progress\nL72: \nL73: Secondary maths (Year 7)\nL74: \nL75: +1\nL76: \nL77: Months' progress\nL78: ## Were the schools in the trial similar to my school?\nL79: Ark Mathematics Mastery aims to improve the quality of maths teaching. Features of the programme that are informed by evidence include a systematic approach to mathematical language, frequent use of objects and pictures to represent mathematical concepts, and an emphasis on high expectations. The EEF funded these trials in 2012 to learn if the approach improved maths attainment for all pupils, while also narrowing the attainment gap between lower and higher attaining pupils.\nL80: Two randomised controlled trials An RCT is used evaluate an educational programme by assigning settings to one of two groups: the intervention group, who receive the programme or the control group, who continue with business as usual. This ensures that any differences in outcomes can be confidently attributed to the programme, providing a robust estimate of the impact and contributing to the evidence for what works in improving educational outcomes.\nL81: were funded, one in primary schools (delivered to year 1 pupils) and one in secondary schools (delivered to year 7 pupils).\nL82: Both evaluations assessed the programme’s impact in its first year of adoption. In subsequent years it was intended that schools would begin to use the approach with older year groups until it was in place across all year groups. The education charity Ark provided participating schools with training and resources to support the adoption of the programme.\nL83: Our trial of the Ark Mathematics Mastery Primary School programme involved 5,108 pupils in 90 schools. The independent evaluation found that pupils in schools adopting Ark Mathematics Mastery made, on average, two months’ progress compared to other pupils in comparison schools. These results have a moderate-to-high security rating: 3 out of 5 on the EEF padlock scale.\nL84: It was not possible to assess whether the programme had a different impact on pupils eligible for free school meals compared to their peers.\nL85: Our trial of the Ark Mathematics Mastery Secondary School programme involved 7,712 pupils in 40 schools. The independent evaluation found that pupils in schools adopting Ark Mathematics Mastery made, on average, one months’ progress compared to other pupils in comparison schools. These results have a high security rating: 4 out of 5 on the EEF padlock scale. A similar average impact was found for pupils eligible for free school meals.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUsing a Randomized Experiment to Compare Mastery Learning Thresholds (https://jedm.educationaldatamining.org/index.php/JEDM/article/download/900/253)\nciteturn19view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 29; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn18view2\",\"pattern\":\"5. EXPERIMENTAL\"}); Total lines: 1156\nL262@P6: 5. EXPERIMENTAL SETUP\nL263@P6: Beginning in April 2023, for all ALEKS products, the assignment of the mastery threshold\nL264@P6: was altered according to the following procedure. Whenever a student starts learning a topic,\nL265@P6: 5% of the time the topic is randomly assigned the high mastery threshold, another 5% of the\nL266@P6: time the topic is randomly assigned the low mastery threshold, and the remaining 90% of the\nL267@P6: time there is no change—that is, the topic is not part of the experiment, and it instead uses the\nL268@P6: mastery threshold normally assigned by the system following the initial assessment. To run our\nL269@P6: analyses, we collected data from April 2023 through November 2024. Although we do not have\nL270@P6: access to demographic information on ALEKS users, overall, the program is used at a wide\nL271@P6: variety of colleges and K–12 schools, mainly in the U.S., with a total user base of over 7 million\nL272@P6: students. ALEKS products cover subjects such as mathematics, chemistry, and statistics, with\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUsing a Randomized Experiment to Compare Mastery Learning Thresholds (https://jedm.educationaldatamining.org/index.php/JEDM/article/download/900/253)\nciteturn19view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 29; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn18view2\",\"pattern\":\"million\"}); Total lines: 1156\nL268@P6: mastery threshold normally assigned by the system following the initial assessment. To run our\nL269@P6: analyses, we collected data from April 2023 through November 2024. Although we do not have\nL270@P6: access to demographic information on ALEKS users, overall, the program is used at a wide\nL271@P6: variety of colleges and K–12 schools, mainly in the U.S., with a total user base of over 7 million\nL272@P6: students. ALEKS products cover subjects such as mathematics, chemistry, and statistics, with\nL273@P6: mathematics being the most popular, followed by chemistry. Finally, appropriate consents are\nL274@P6: collected and notice provided to all our users via our Terms of Service and Privacy Notice, which\nL275@P6: specify the use of the anonymized data for product improvements and research purposes.\nL276@P6: While our modeling procedure and analysis of the data closely follow the methodology used\nL277@P6: in Matayoshi et al. (2022), for completeness we next describe this methodology in detail. To\nL361@P9: is taken. Because of this, we categorize the topics based on their status (a) at the time a progress\nL362@P9: assessment is taken and (b) at the end of our data collection period.4 Using these categorizations,\nL363@P9: Table 2 shows the summary statistics for the two mastery thresholds, using a data set containing\nL364@P9: almost 33 million data points.5 We can see that the high mastery threshold, with a learning ratio\nL365@P9: of 0.789, is more difficult in comparison to the low mastery threshold, which has a learning\nL366@P9: ratio of 0.809. Furthermore, the high mastery threshold has slightly larger proportions in the\nL367@P9: Fail, Incomplete, and No Response categories. Finally, Table 2 also shows the mean and median\nL368@P9: times for the learning sequences, with these times being larger for the high mastery threshold.\nL369@P9: Overall, these results are sensible, as we expect it to be more difficult and require more time for\nL370@P9: students to learn topics with the high mastery threshold. However, the differences are relatively\nL419@P10: be a difficult task, and in what follows we do not attempt to do so. From a purely scientific\nL420@P10: perspective, this is suboptimal, as it would be interesting to precisely understand the relationship\nL421@P10: between the amount of practice and the retention of knowledge. However, we are still able to\nL422@P10: analyze the overall differences between the mastery learning thresholds, without worrying about\nL423@P10: which of the above factors specifically are responsible for these differences. Importantly, we\nL424@P10: believe that this approach is more useful for designing and improving adaptive learning and\nL425@P10: intelligent tutoring systems, as it follows the behavior of the mastery thresholds in an actual\nL426@P10: production system.\nL427@P10: With these caveats in mind, we begin by extracting extra problem data from April 2023\nL428@P10: through November 2024. After processing the data to remove any extra problems that are not\nL429@P10: part of the experiment, we are left with slightly less than two million data points. Next, because\nL430@P10: we want to include the student’s performance on the initial assessment as one of our control\nL431@P10: 6Based on 5,000 cluster bootstrap samples, the 95% confidence intervals for the outcome proportions are all\nL432@P10: less than 0.0015 in width, while the 95% confidence intervals for the duration values are all less than 0.06 in width.\nL433@P10: 11\nL434@P10-11: 318 Journal of Educational Data Mining, Volume 17, No 1, 2025Table 4: Comparison of learning sequence statistics for topics in the high mastery and low\nL435@P11: mastery groups.\nL436@P11: High mastery Low mastery\nL437@P11: (692,780) (711,945)\nL438@P11: Correct answers 4.2 (3) 2.7 (2)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUsing a Randomized Experiment to Compare Mastery Learning Thresholds (https://jedm.educationaldatamining.org/index.php/JEDM/article/download/900/253)\nciteturn19view5 [wor